Problem 1: Feed-Forward Networks Are Wasteful

In standard Transformers:

Every token

Goes through

The same big FFN

This becomes absurdly expensive at scale.

DeepSeek Solution 1: Mixture of Experts (MoE)
Core Idea

Instead of one giant FFN, use many smaller experts, but activate only a few per token.

Mathematically:

MoE(x) = Σ gi(x) · Experti(x)


Where:

Experti = specialized subnetworks

gi(x) = gating network deciding which experts matter

Only top-k experts run.

Why This Is Powerful

🧠 Experts specialize

⚡ Computation is sparse

📈 Parameters scale without compute exploding

You get:

Billions of parameters

Without billions of FLOPs per token

DeepSeek-Specific Improvements

DeepSeek adds:

Fine-grained expert segmentation

Shared expert isolation

Load-balancing strategies

These prevent:

Expert collapse

Training instability

Uneven expert usage

Mental Model

Think of it like:

A hospital with many specialists

Each patient only sees 1–2 doctors

Not the entire hospital staff

Problem 2: KV Cache Is the Real Enemy

During inference, Transformers must store:

Keys (K)

Values (V)

For every token, every layer.

Memory cost:

~ 2 × n × dmodel × nlayers


At GPT-4 scale:

This becomes hundreds of GB to TB

Memory bandwidth, not compute, becomes the bottleneck

DeepSeek Solution 2: Multi-Head Latent Attention (MLA)
Core Insight

Keys and Values are redundant.

So DeepSeek:

Compresses K and V together

Stores only a low-rank latent representation

Reconstructs what’s needed on the fly

How It Works (Intuition)

Instead of storing:

Full K and V vectors

Store:

A compressed latent vector cKV

Using:

Down-projection (compress)

Up-projection (recover)

So:

K ≈ WDKV · WUK
V ≈ WDKV · WUV


This is joint compression, not separate.

Why This Matters

KV cache size drops by ~8–16×

Inference becomes feasible for long contexts

GPU memory is no longer the choke point

Real Numbers (This Is Huge)

From the paper:

Model	Normal KV Cache	DeepSeek MLA
GPT-3.5	~13 GB	~1.6 GB
GPT-4	~64 GB	~4 GB
GPT-4 Turbo (128K)	~1 TB	~67 GB

That’s the difference between:

“Impossible”

and “Deployable”

Problem 3: Positional Embeddings Break Compression

RoPE (Rotary Position Embedding) is great for long context.

But:

It entangles position with content

This breaks DeepSeek’s compression trick

Because:

Matrix multiplication is not commutative

You can’t absorb RoPE cleanly into compressed matrices

DeepSeek Solution 3: Decoupled RoPE
Key Insight

Separate:

Content

Position

Instead of mixing them into the same vector.

What They Do

They introduce:

Separate RoPE-only queries and keys

Concatenate them with compressed content vectors

So attention becomes:

q·k = (content part) + (position part)


This preserves:

Compression efficiency

Long-range dependency modeling

Fast inference

Mental Model

Think of:

Content = what is being said

Position = where it is said

DeepSeek says:

“Stop tying these together.”

